d wearing this like  here and then the hat but early  assistants made an incredible recovery  despite missing out on two years of  school I couldn't even go to the garden  mister times I couldn't leave my room so  which is pretty much confined to the  house really mom had to stay home she  had to stop working because she didn't  trust me to stay on my own if she was in  a room I wouldn't go into it and what  was that well because I felt I got so  bad that I felt okay no it's not just  other people it's my parents as well  they can't see me because I'm too ugly  for my parents to see me thank you  okay in southeast London Emily arrives  at the Maudsley clinic and today it's  all about taking her out of her comfort  zone therapist Amita wants to see how  far she's come so know this is hard but  really try and challenge yourself as  much as you can and the challenge today  for Emily is to remove her makeup and to  head outside  okhla him  okay so have a look around you and see  if people are still looking  she's conscious that people are staring  at her thinking that she's ugly I feel  like in this situation it's even more  challenging because obviously  we are sort of drawing attention to  ourselves  yeah so that's making me anxious she  gets to the end of the road before  panicking it's too busy she decides to  go back inside it just made me honestly  have like a panic attack  I felt really insecure a little bit  hopeless in a way and consultant Amita  has dealt with hundreds of patients like  Emily over the years we've had lots of  young people come in and they've come a  flood kind of the whole face so people  we've assessed where they've had kind of  towels on their heads because they just  can't even be bear to been seen by even  clinicians that are experts in BDD the  Mosley hospital is now going into  schools along with the mental health  charity rethink to raise awareness and  to carry out research so far their  analysis of around 600 11 to 16 year  olds has found that whilst 80% were  concerned with at least one part of  their body up to five percent could have  BDD many people with body dysmorphic  disorder wait years for a diagnosis in  when I was just talking to right now I  really wanted to look in the mirror I'm  sure people are thinking that's cause  she's vain  it's because I'm really worried that all  these people who are watching this are  gonna think you know she's really ugly  but in time people learn to manage their  symptoms it's the way that I'm coping  with it is differently so I'm thinking  you know so what who's gonna look at me  and are they with people looking at me  you know are they important in my life  and the likes of aleus give others hope  it is in your head and with enough  therapy like with the right therapy you  will get better and it will just you  will improve and you will go back to  normal life again there are high suicide  rates for those living with BDD some  refuse to get help for fear that they'll  be considered vain but with greater  awareness and research his hope more  youngsters will in future be diagnosed  earlier

